>
  <div class="thoughts-page min-h-screen">
    <article class="essay max-w-[728px] mx-auto px-6 py-16">

      <EssayHeader slug="whose-egg-is-that" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> Companies should abolish the HR layer that screens job candidates and give the decision back to the managers and executives who will work with the hire, because people who never live with the result judge badly and the skill of judging fades without use.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">A previous essay of mine, "<a href="/thoughts/inventors-of-problems" class="underline hover:opacity-70 t-amber">Inventors of Problems</a>," argued that the people function (HR) is a supplier rather than a culprit, and that the invoice for its excesses is signed at the top. That is true, but it is an unsatisfying place to stop, because a diagnosis that ends in shared responsibility tends to end in nothing. So here is the specific proposal, stated without hedging, followed by an honest account of where it breaks.</p>
        <p><strong>Abolish the layer that evaluates candidates.</strong> Not the payroll team, not the benefits administrators, not the immigration filings, and not the two serious investigators every large firm needs and rarely has. The layer that reads the resumes, runs the screens, owns the scoring rubric, administers the values questionnaire and holds the veto: that one goes. The evaluation returns to the operator who will have to work with the result. For senior roles that means the chief executive and the chief operating officer sitting in the room themselves, on the record, with their names attached to the outcome.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-060044/arctic-60n-140e/preview-1200.webp"
          width="874"
          height="1148"
          alt="Abstract painting of broad pink, coral and green fields cut by a heavy black diagonal stroke"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          60N 140E, Arctic, rendered from that day's atmospheric data.
          From <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-060044/arctic-60n-140e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70 break-all">art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-060044/arctic-60n-140e</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Person Deciding Does Not Pay</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Every bad institution can be described by one sentence about who bears the consequences. In hiring the sentence is short: the person who decides which candidates get seen will never work with any of them.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Consider what that arrangement asks. A coordinator with no experience of the craft in question is handed two hundred applications and a job requisition written by a manager in a hurry, and is asked to reduce the pile to eight. Nothing in the coordinator's training, incentives or working life equips them to recognize the third-best structural engineer in the state, or the trader who has a real edge, or the nurse who is calm in the worst twenty minutes of somebody's life. So they do the only thing the position permits. They match strings: years in seat, title held, school attended, keyword present, gap explained, degree required even where the degree is decorative.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The result is a filter tuned to the qualities that are cheap to fake and cheap to acquire, and blind to the ones that are neither. The people who game it get through. The people who spent those years getting good at something instead of getting good at describing it do not. This is not a moral failing on anyone's part. It is what happens when you ask someone to grade an exam in a subject they have not studied, and then tell them they will never meet the students.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the manager who will live with the hire, who knows exactly which two paragraphs of a resume matter and could sort the pile properly in ninety minutes, is not allowed to see the pile. They receive the eight. They are told this is efficient. It is efficient in the same sense that not reading the loan documents is efficient.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Rejection Is a Skill, and It Decays</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">A piece of natural history describes this better than any management literature.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The common cuckoo does not raise its young. It lays a single egg in the nest of another species. The host, most famously the reed warbler, incubates the egg, feeds the chick, and raises the chick that has already pushed the host's own eggs over the rim. The arms race between the two is among the best documented in biology. The cuckoo's egg mimics the host's in color and speckling, sometimes with startling accuracy. The host's counter is the ability to look at its own clutch and reject the egg that does not belong.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The detail that matters is what happens to that ability when it goes unused. Populations of the same host species on islands or in regions where the cuckoo has been absent for a long time are markedly worse at rejection. They accept eggs that a parasitized population would throw out immediately. The skill is not free and not permanent. It is maintained by exercise, it costs something to keep, and where the pressure lifts it fades within a manageable number of generations. Reintroduce the parasite and the naive population is defenseless for a while. The birds are not stupid. Nobody in that lineage has had to look closely at an egg in a very long time.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">A firm that has outsourced candidate evaluation for fifteen years is that island population. Its managers have not had to look closely at an egg. Ask a director in such a company to run a hiring process from a blank page and watch what happens. They will ask for the rubric, request the standard question bank, wonder aloud whether they are allowed to ask that, and end by deferring to whoever seemed most confident in the debrief. The capability has not been suppressed by policy. It has atrophied from disuse. This is the real cost of the arrangement, larger than any single bad hire, because it is the cost of a firm that has forgotten how to tell what belongs in its own nest.</p>
        <p>This also answers the obvious objection that executives are not trained recruiters. Correct. Nobody is born a trained sailor either. Judgment about people is the central competence of running a company, and a chief executive who cannot exercise it without a facilitator has a problem that no amount of process will solve, only conceal.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Screen That Selects for Agreement</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Now the part that people prefer to argue about in bad faith.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Somewhere in the last decade a second filter was added on top of the first. It goes by several names. It usually shows up as a required statement, a values rubric, a set of scored questions about contribution to an inclusive culture, or a "culture fit" dimension in the debrief that any interviewer may invoke without evidence. The stated purpose is to widen the pool. The actual function is to test whether the candidate can produce a paragraph in a particular register.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">My objection is narrow. It is not an argument against treating people decently, and it is emphatically not an argument for reopening the question of whether race, sex, religion or age may be used against a candidate. Those questions are settled, and settled correctly. Any executive who reads this as a license to relitigate them has badly misread it and should expect the full attention of the law. The objection is to a screen that scores a candidate on fluency in an idiom rather than capability in a job, and therefore does the opposite of what it advertises.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Consider who actually clears such a filter. Someone who spent four years at an institution where the vocabulary is native. Someone with the time and coaching to prepare a polished statement. Someone who has been taught, formally, which words carry credit this season. Now consider who does not: the candidate who came up through a trade, the immigrant who is superb at the work and awkward in the register, the person who did not go to the kind of school where this is a spoken language, the one who declines on principle to perform a belief on demand. The screen was sold as an instrument of access and it functions as a class filter with a conscience. It selects for the credentialed and articulate, who are exactly the people who needed no help getting through the door.</p>
        <p>The second cost falls on the firm, and it is the one that should concern a board. A screen for agreement produces agreement. Run it for five years and you have assembled a room in which everyone shares the same premises. That is a pleasant room to work in and a catastrophic one in which to assess a risk. Firms do not usually die of too little harmony. They die because nobody in the meeting was constitutionally capable of saying that the thing did not work.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-04-060044/western-pacific-15n-120e/preview-1200.webp"
